<Guide lang="en" slug="retiring-to-spain" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="renovatie" ctaTitle="Getting the house ready for later life?" ctaText="We adapt your home so you can live in it comfortably for years: a walk-in shower, fewer steps, better insulation. With a fixed price up front.">
  <p>Spain remains the favourite retirement destination for Britons. The sunshine, the slower pace and a large English-speaking community make the Axarquía and the Costa del Sol especially popular. Since Brexit there is more paperwork, but with good preparation your pension, healthcare and new home are well within reach. Tax and immigration rules change regularly, so have your own plans checked by an adviser who knows both the UK and Spain.</p>

  <h2 id="visa">Staying longer than 90 days</h2>
  <p>British citizens can spend 90 days in every 180 in the Schengen area without a visa. Since October 2025, those days are recorded at the border by the EU’s Entry/Exit System. To live in Spain you need a visa. For retirees the usual route is the <strong>non-lucrative visa</strong>: you show a guaranteed passive income (around €2,400 a month for the main applicant and €600 for each family member, based on 2025 figures), private health insurance and a clean criminal record. You apply at the Spanish consulate in the UK before you move. All the requirements are in <a href="/en/guides/non-lucrative-visa-spain/">the non-lucrative visa guide</a>.</p>
  <p>Britons who were already legally resident in Spain before 2021 are protected by the Withdrawal Agreement and keep their rights.</p>

  <h2 id="state-pension">Your UK State Pension</h2>
  <p>The Department for Work and Pensions pays your State Pension into a UK or Spanish bank account. In Spain it is increased every year, just as in the UK. Tell the International Pension Centre that you are moving. If you move before State Pension age and have gaps in your National Insurance record, you may be able to pay voluntary contributions. The rules for people abroad were tightened in 2026, so check your options with HMRC before you leave.</p>

  <h2 id="private">Private and workplace pensions</h2>
  <p>Workplace and private pensions can also be paid out while you live in Spain. Tell your provider your new address and apply to HMRC to stop UK tax being deducted once the pension is taxable in Spain. Be careful with lump sums: the UK tax-free lump sum is not automatically tax free in Spain if you take it while you are Spanish tax resident. Many people take it before they move. ISAs also lose their tax-free status in Spain.</p>

  <h2 id="tax">Where do you pay tax?</h2>
  <p>Once you spend more than 183 days a year in Spain, you are Spanish tax resident and taxed on your worldwide income. Under the UK–Spain tax treaty, the State Pension and private pensions are normally taxed in Spain. Government service pensions usually stay taxable in the UK, but must still be declared in Spain.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Spanish income tax (<em>IRPF</em>) is progressive, with rates from around 19% to 47%. On a middle-sized pension that can be more than in the UK. Work it out before you move.</li>
    <li>You file each year between April and the end of June (<em>modelo 100</em>).</li>
    <li>If you hold more than €50,000 in bank accounts, investments or property outside Spain, you declare it once with <em>modelo 720</em>, and again after large changes.</li>
    <li>Andalucía gives a 100% reduction on wealth tax. Only above €3 million does a national solidarity tax still apply.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The taxes on your house itself, such as IBI, are in the <a href="/en/guides/spanish-property-tax-calendar/">Spanish property tax calendar</a>. What happens when you die is covered in <a href="/en/guides/inheritance-tax-spain/">inheritance tax in Spain</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="health">Healthcare</h2>
  <p>With a UK State Pension you can register an S1 form, so the UK pays for your Spanish public healthcare. Before you reach State Pension age you usually need private health insurance, which the visa requires anyway. Everything is explained in <a href="/en/guides/health-insurance-spain/">health insurance in Spain</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="home">A home for the years ahead</h2>
  <p>After retiring you may live in the same house for twenty years or more. The home that feels perfect now also has to work when driving or climbing stairs becomes harder. A remote finca on a steep hillside is beautiful, but far from the doctor and the shops. An apartment with a lift or a house on the edge of a village is often easier later on. Compare the options in <a href="/en/guides/finca-apartment-or-village-house/retirees/">the best home for retirees</a>, or read <a href="/en/guides/buying-an-apartment-in-spain/">buying an apartment in Spain</a>.</p>
  <p>Already own a house? A walk-in shower, fewer steps, grab rails and better insulation make it fit for later life. See <a href="/en/guides/accessible-home-spain/">making your home accessible</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="checklist">What to arrange in good time</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Have a tax adviser compare your net income in Spain and the UK before you move.</li>
    <li>Get your <a href="/en/guides/nie-number-spain/">NIE number</a> and a Spanish bank account.</li>
    <li>Make a Spanish will for your Spanish assets.</li>
    <li>Exchange your UK driving licence for a Spanish one within six months of becoming resident.</li>
    <li>Away often? Let us check the house with <a href="/en/key-holding/">key holding</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The full step-by-step move is in <a href="/en/guides/moving-to-spain-from-the-uk/">moving to Spain from the UK</a>.</p>
</Guide>
